course: dictionary
lesson: 8547
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning. The context, meaning the words around them, tells you which meaning the writer wants. The word scallop is a good example: it can name an animal, a food, or a pattern of curves.
explain: «A scallop can have up to about 200 tiny eyes along the edge of its body» is about a sea creature. The next part, «it swims away by clapping its two shells together», confirms it: a scallop has shells, and it is an animal.
explain: «Scalloped hammerheads gather in big schools» is different. Here scalloped is part of the name of a shark, so it is not about shells or curves at all. Always ask which meaning fits the sentence.
explain: When a word has several meanings, a dictionary lists them as numbered senses. Read each one, then put it into the sentence in place of the hard word. The meaning that still makes sense is the right one.
fact: Words with more than one meaning are called multiple-meaning words.
fact: A scallop is a sea creature with two fan-shaped, ridged shells, and it swims by clapping them together.
fact: Scallop can also mean the meat of this animal, eaten as seafood.
fact: A scallop is one of a row of curves along an edge, like on a pie crust or lace. To scallop an edge means to cut it into such curves.
fact: In Victoria and Tasmania, Australia, a «potato scallop» is a slice of potato fried in batter.
exercise: «We ate fried scallops with lemon for dinner.» What is a scallop in this sentence? | *Seafood, the meat of the sea creature | A grey, wet day | A shark with a hammer-shaped head | A row of curves on a pie crust
exercise: «She decided to scallop the edge of the pie crust.» What does scallop mean here? | To eat with a fork | To fry in batter | To swim by clapping the shells together | *To cut the edge into a row of curves
exercise: «Scalloped hammerheads gather in big schools near underwater mountains.» What kind of animal is a scalloped hammerhead? | A row of curves on lace | A slice of potato | A sea creature with two shells | *A shark
test: «A scallop can have about 200 tiny eyes along the edge of its body.» What are a scallop's eyes useful for? | Growing a new shell | *Spotting shadows, such as a hungry starfish | Reading small print | Hearing the waves
test: «Mum made potato scallops for tea in Melbourne.» What is a potato scallop? | A type of shark | A curve along the edge of a pie | A sea creature with two shells | *A slice of potato fried in batter
exam: In history, what did a scallop shell mark? | A dinner of fried fish | A tiny eye on a sea creature | A pie crust with a fancy edge | *A badge of pilgrims walking to Santiago de Compostela
